  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nLatest visible satellite imagery shows cumulus clouds developing \nthanks to diurnal heating over the central and western U.P., with \nscattered midlevel cloud cover across Lake Superior. The latest \nwater vapor imagery shows a fairly dry zonally-oriented airmass \nacross the U.P. and much of the eastern US-CA border, which is \nreflective of the generally zonal flow across the area at 500mb. \nLows tonight are expected to fall into the mid to low 40s, which \ncould result in some patchy fog in the southwestern U.P.\n\nAs the 500mb low currently over northern Manitoba continues to \nprogress eastward this evening, 500mb ridging is expected to build \nover central Canada. Combined with the effects of the high pressure \ncenter currently centered over AR, this will result in ridging over \nthe central US and generally northwesterly upper level flow across \nthe western Great Lakes region tonight through Friday morning. A \nshortwave trough is expected to move across the largescale ridge to \nour west tonight, arriving just south of the CWA by Friday afternoon \nand bringing our next best chance of rain. The NBM continues to keep \nthe heaviest rainfall south of the U.P., which is in agreeance with \nthe latest CAMs guidance. LREF chances for 24-hour rain totals \ngreater than 0.01 inches between 00z Saturday and 00z Sunday are \nbetween 20-40% for the northern and eastern U.P., and increase to \nupwards of 60% for the central southern U.P. Probabilities decrease \nsignificantly with increasing totals, with a 10-25% chance of totals \nexceeding 0.5 inches for Menominee County.\n\nOn Sunday, a 500mb ridge is expected to build over MN in advance of \nan eastward-moving 500mb low currently over northern CA. This ridge \nwill progress over the area Monday, bringing clearing skies. \nTuesday, the aforementioned low weakens due to the influence of the \nridge as it progresses into our region and split flow develops \naround the weakening low and amplifying ridge.At this time, there is \nstill some uncertainty as to the exact track of this split flow, \nhowever, the U.P. is currently projected to be under generally high \npressure, which would result in dry weather and seasonable \ntemperatures through midweek.",
